IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `eia-core`
IMPORT `ei7-related-nouns`

-- Employment Insurance Act (S.C. 1996, c. 23): RELATED PROVISIONS of 2020 to 2023 (src:3888-3999): 2020, c. 5, s. 60; ten
-- Interim Orders of 2020; 2021, c. 23, ss. 331 to 336; 2022, c. 10, ss. 406 and 411; 2023, c. 26, ss. 664 and 676.
-- Written by piece ei7 (Sonnet 5.5). Text: the unofficial plain-text rendering of the Justice Laws consolidation
-- current to 2026-09-30; nothing here claims to be verified against the official text.
-- Seat: the person who has lost work or cannot work, asking which version of the Act governs their benefit period.
-- A day fixed under another section of an amending Act, or "the day on which ... come into force", is not in the
-- deposited texts: it is a DATE input of the rule that needs it. The earlier text that "continues to apply" is not in
-- the deposited texts either; a rule says that it governs and which provisions, not what they say.

§ `2020, c. 5, s. 60 - section 58 as it read before the day fixed under section 61`

-- Fork F13: the extract does not say which Act "section 58" belongs to; the rule says only that it continues to apply, as it
-- read immediately before the day fixed under section 61, to a claimant whose benefit period begins before that day.
-- src:3888 | — 2020, c. 5, s. 60
-- src:3889 | Transitional provision
-- src:3890 | 60 Section 58, as it read immediately before the day fixed under section 61, continues to apply in respect of any claimant whose benefit period begins before that day.
@ref Employment Insurance Act, related provision of 2020, c. 5, s. 60 (src:3890)
GIVEN `the first day of the benefit period` IS A DATE  -- out of range: section 10(1), the day a benefit period begins (piece ei1); section 152.11 for a self-employed person (piece ei5)
      `the day fixed under section 61` IS A DATE  -- out of range: a day fixed by order or by another section of the amending Act; not in the deposited texts (no piece)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Act RP 2020 c. 5 s. 60 section 58 as it read immediately before the day fixed under section 61 continues to apply in respect of the claimant` IF
    `the first day of the benefit period` LESS THAN `the day fixed under section 61`

§ `Interim Orders of 2020 - "applies despite"`

-- SOR/2020-61, s. 2: "The provisions of Part VIII.4 ... apply despite any other provision of that Act or any of its
-- regulations." The rule answers JUST TRUE where the other provision is one of those; for any other conflicting
-- provision (another Act, say) the section says nothing: NOTHING. Part VIII.4 itself is piece ei6's.
-- src:3892 | — SOR/2020-61, s. 2
-- src:3893 | 2 The provisions of Part VIII.4 of the Employment Insurance Act apply despite any other provision of that Act or any of its regulations.
@ref Employment Insurance Act, related provision of SOR/2020-61, s. 2 (src:3893)
GIVEN `the other provision is a provision of the Employment Insurance Act outside Part VIII.4, or of any of its regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Act RP SOR/2020-61 s. 2 the provisions of Part VIII.4 apply despite the other provision` IS
    IF `the other provision is a provision of the Employment Insurance Act outside Part VIII.4, or of any of its regulations`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- Nine more Interim Orders say the same thing of themselves: "For greater certainty, this Interim Order applies despite
-- any provision of the Employment Insurance Act or any of its regulations." One rule serves the nine. The orders are
-- SOR/2020-88 s. 5, -89 s. 4, -95 s. 3, -141 s. 3, -169 s. 7, -173 s. 7, -187 s. 5, -188 s. 7 and -208 s. 8; their own
-- text is not among the inputs, so what each order provides is not encoded. Fork F14.
